From 378628506e19af724bcdb14f46198ad3dd37a786 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 20:34:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Bluetooth: hci_qca: do not fail system suspend for a
 controller that never came up

qca_suspend() waits for QCA_IBS_DISABLED to clear. That flag is set while
a firmware download is in flight and cleared when one completes, so on a
controller whose setup never completed it is set and stays set. The wait
then always runs its full FW_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS and returns -ETIMEDOUT:

  Bluetooth: hci0: SSR or FW download time out
  hci_uart_qca serial0-0: PM: dpm_run_callback(): qca_suspend returns -110
  hci_uart_qca serial0-0: PM: failed to suspend: error -110
  PM: Some devices failed to suspend, or early wake event detected

The measured cost is 3.3 s per attempt, and every system suspend on the
machine is abandoned -- for a Bluetooth controller that was never usable
in the first place.

If hci_uart_open() never ran there is no in-band sleep to negotiate, so
return early. The existing QCA_ROM_FW and QCA_BT_OFF cases already take
this shape.

Signed-off-by: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
index 976f099fcb1ba..311778e4974fc 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_qca.c
@@ -2603,6 +2603,17 @@ static int __maybe_unused qca_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	u8 cmd;
 	u32 wait_timeout = 0;
 
+	/* If the controller never came up, hci_uart_open() never ran and
+	 * there is no in-band sleep to negotiate. QCA_IBS_DISABLED is set
+	 * while firmware downloads and cleared when one completes, so on a
+	 * controller that never completed setup it stays set forever and the
+	 * wait below always runs its full FW_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS before
+	 * returning -ETIMEDOUT. That turns a dead Bluetooth controller into a
+	 * failed system suspend for the whole machine.
+	 */
+	if (!test_bit(HCI_UART_PROTO_READY, &hu->flags))
+		return 0;
+
 	set_bit(QCA_SUSPENDING, &qca->flags);
 
 	/* if BT SoC is running with default firmware then it does not
